Creating an advanced `awk` program to handle the described tasks requires careful planning and structuring of the code. Below is a sample `awk` script that attempts to fulfill the requirements. Please note that this script assumes that the input files are in a CSV format with headers and that the area units are specified in the data (e.g., "sq mi" for square miles, "sq km" for square kilometers).

```awk
#!/usr/bin/awk -f

BEGIN {
    FS = ","; # Assuming CSV files with comma as field separator
    OFS = ",";
    print "Continent,Total Customers,Average Area (sq km)" > "output.csv";
    error_log = "error.log";
}

# Function to normalize continent names
function normalize_continent(continent) {
    gsub(/ /, "", continent);
    return tolower(continent);
}

# Function to convert area to square kilometers
function convert_to_sq_km(area, unit) {
    if (unit == "sq mi") {
        return area * 2.58999; # Convert square miles to square kilometers
    } else if (unit == "sq km") {
        return area; # Already in square kilometers
    } else {
        print "Unknown area unit: " unit > error_log;
        return -1; # Error code for unknown unit
    }
}

{
    if (FNR == 1) {
        next; # Skip header lines
    }

    # Normalize data
    continent = normalize_continent($1);
    area = convert_to_sq_km($3, $4);
    if (area == -1) {
        next; # Skip this record due to error
    }

    # Aggregate data
    customers[continent] += $2;
    area_sum[continent] += area;
    area_count[continent]++;

    # Track historical data for growth trend
    year = $5;
    customers_per_year[continent, year] += $2;
}

END {
    # Calculate averages and sort
    for (cont in customers) {
        split(cont, cont_split, SUBSEP);
        continent = cont_split[1];
        average_area = area_sum[continent] / area_count[continent];
        print continent, customers[continent], average_area > "temp.csv";
    }

    # Sort the output
    cmd = "sort -t',' -k2,2nr -k3,3n temp.csv >> output.csv";
    system(cmd);

    # Clean up temporary file
    system("rm temp.csv");

    # Print historical growth trend (not sorted, for simplicity)
    print "Continent,Year,Total Customers" > "growth_trend.csv";
    for (key in customers_per_year) {
        split(key, key_split, SUBSEP);
        print key_split[1], key_split[2], customers_per_year[key] > "growth_trend.csv";
    }
}

```

To run this script, save it to a file (e.g., `process_customers.awk`), make it executable with `chmod +x process_customers.awk`, and then execute it by passing the customer data files as arguments:

```bash
./process_customers.awk customer_data1.csv customer_data2.csv ...
```

This script will generate an `output.csv` file with the sorted results, a `growth_trend.csv` file with the historical growth trend, and an `error.log` file with any errors encountered during processing.

Please note that this script is a starting point and may require adjustments based on the actual format and content of your data files. Additionally, for very large datasets, `awk` might not be the most efficient tool, and you might want to consider using a database system or a programming language better suited for handling large datasets, such as Python with pandas.

