To improve the lower envelope for rising data, we can modify the method to use local minima instead of the global minimum. This approach will allow the envelope to more closely follow the shape of the data, especially in areas where the data points rise or fall sharply. Here's how we can implement this:

1. We will divide the x-axis into segments and find the local minimum y-value within each segment. This will give us a set of local minima that we can use to construct the lower envelope.

2. We will then interpolate between these local minima to create a smooth curve that serves as the lower envelope.

Here's the modified code:

```mathematica
ClearAll[allXValues, localMin, interpolateLocalMin, nearestMax]
allXValues = Sort[pts[[;; , 1]]];
nearestMax = {#, First[Nearest[pts, {#, Max[pts[[;; , -1]]]}]][[-1]]} & /@ allXValues;

(* Define the number of segments *)
numSegments = 50;
segmentSize = (Max[allXValues] - Min[allXValues]) / numSegments;

(* Find local minima *)
localMin = Table[
  With[{segmentPts = Select[pts, x1 <= #[[1]] < x1 + segmentSize &]},
    {x1 + segmentSize/2, Min[segmentPts[[All, 2]]]}
  ],
  {x1, Min[allXValues], Max[allXValues] - segmentSize, segmentSize}
];

(* Interpolate between local minima *)
interpolateLocalMin = Interpolation[localMin, InterpolationOrder -> 1];

(* Generate the lower envelope *)
lowerEnvelope = Table[{x, interpolateLocalMin[x]}, {x, Min[allXValues], Max[allXValues], segmentSize/10}];

ListLinePlot[{nearestMax, lowerEnvelope}, Epilog -> {Gray, Point[pts]}]
```

In this code, `numSegments` defines how many segments we divide the x-axis into. You can adjust this number to change the granularity of the local minima calculation. The `InterpolationOrder` parameter in the `Interpolation` function can also be adjusted to change the smoothness of the lower envelope curve.

By using local minima, the lower envelope will now more accurately follow the contours of the data, providing a better fit for rising and falling trends within the scatter plot.

