To make the validator dynamically adapt to changes in job titles and salary ranges, you can externalize the configuration of job titles and salary ranges to a database or a configuration file that can be updated without changing the code. Here's how you can modify the validators to fetch this dynamic configuration:

### Step 1: Externalize Configuration

Store the job titles and salary ranges in a database or a configuration file. For example, you could have a properties file like this:

```properties
# job-titles.properties
job.titles=Software Engineer,Product Manager,Data Analyst

# salary-ranges.properties
salary.range.Software Engineer=50000,150000
salary.range.Product Manager=60000,160000
salary.range.Data Analyst=45000,120000
```

Or a database table with columns for job title, minimum salary, and maximum salary.

### Step 2: Update Validators to Use Configuration

Modify the validators to read from the externalized configuration instead of hard-coded values.

```java
// Validator for job titles
public class JobTitleValidator implements ConstraintValidator<JobTitle, String> {
    private List<String> validTitles;

    @Override
    public void initialize(JobTitle constraintAnnotation) {
        // Load job titles from a configuration file or database
        validTitles = ConfigurationLoader.loadJobTitles();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isValid(String value, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
        return validTitles.contains(value);
    }
}

// Validator for salary
public class SalaryValidator implements ConstraintValidator<Salary, BigDecimal> {
    private Map<String, Range<BigDecimal>> salaryRanges;

