To estimate the parameters of a LogNormalDistribution with a given confidence interval in Mathematica, you would typically use a method such as maximum likelihood estimation (MLE). The number of data points required to achieve a certain confidence interval width depends on the underlying variability of the data and the desired precision of the parameter estimates.

The LogNormalDistribution in Mathematica has two parameters: the mean (μ) and standard deviation (σ) of the distribution's logarithm. To estimate these parameters with a certain level of confidence, you would collect sample data from the distribution and then use Mathematica's built-in functions to fit a LogNormalDistribution to the data and compute confidence intervals for the parameters.

There is no straightforward formula to determine the exact number of data points needed for a specific confidence interval width without knowing the underlying distribution's variability. However, as a rule of thumb, larger sample sizes will generally lead to narrower confidence intervals, assuming the data is representative of the underlying distribution.

To get a rough estimate of the number of data points needed, you could perform a simulation study in Mathematica. You would generate sample data from a LogNormalDistribution with known parameters, fit a distribution to the sample data, estimate the parameters and their confidence intervals, and repeat this process many times to see how the confidence interval width varies with the sample size.

Here's a basic outline of how you might set up such a simulation in Mathematica:

```mathematica
(* Set the true parameters of the LogNormalDistribution *)
trueMu = 0;
trueSigma = 1;

(* Define the number of simulations and the sample size *)
numSimulations = 1000;
sampleSize = 100; (* Start with a sample size of 100 and adjust as needed *)

(* Perform the simulations *)
simulationResults = Table[
  Module[{sample, estimatedDistribution, ciMu, ciSigma},
    (* Generate a sample *)
    sample = RandomVariate[LogNormalDistribution[trueMu, trueSigma], sampleSize];

    (* Fit the LogNormalDistribution to the sample *)
    estimatedDistribution = EstimatedDistribution[sample, LogNormalDistribution[mu, sigma]];

    (* Compute the 95% confidence intervals for the parameters *)
    ciMu = ConfidenceInterval[EstimatedDistribution[sample, LogNormalDistribution[mu, sigma], ParameterEstimator -> {"MaximumLikelihood", Method -> "NMaximize"}], mu];
    ciSigma = ConfidenceInterval[EstimatedDistribution[sample, LogNormalDistribution[mu, sigma], ParameterEstimator -> {"MaximumLikelihood", Method -> "NMaximize"}], sigma];

    (* Return the confidence intervals *)
    {ciMu, ciSigma}
  ],
  {numSimulations}
];

(* Analyze the simulation results to determine the average width of the confidence intervals *)
averageCIWidthMu = Mean[#[[2]] - #[[1]] & /@ (First /@ simulationResults)];
averageCIWidthSigma = Mean[#[[2]] - #[[1]] & /@ (Last /@ simulationResults)];

(* Output the average confidence interval widths *)
{averageCIWidthMu, averageCIWidthSigma}
```

This code will give you the average width of the confidence intervals for both parameters across the simulations. You can adjust the `sampleSize` variable and rerun the simulations to see how the confidence interval widths change. You would increase the sample size until the average confidence interval widths are narrow enough to meet your desired 5% confidence interval criterion.

