One limitation of Bayesian inference in the context of time-series data is the computational complexity. Time-series models often involve dependencies between observations, which can make the models more complex and the inference process more computationally intensive. Additionally, specifying appropriate priors for time-series models can be challenging, as it requires careful consideration of the temporal dependencies in the data. Another limitation is that Bayesian methods can be sensitive to the choice of priors, especially in the case of small data sets or when the data does not provide strong information about the parameters.

