In Bison, `YYSTYPE` is the type of the semantic values of the tokens and nonterminals. By default, `YYSTYPE` is an `int`, but you can redefine it to be any type you want, including a C++ class or a union type.

To declare `YYSTYPE` in Bison for C++, you typically use the `%union` directive to define a union that includes the various types of values that your grammar symbols can have. Then, you use the `%type` directive to specify the type of each nonterminal, and the `%token` directive to specify the type of each token that has a semantic value.

Here's an example of how to declare `YYSTYPE` in Bison for a C++ parser:

```bison
%{
#include <string>
// Include other necessary headers or declarations
%}

%union {
    int int_val;
    double double_val;
    std::string* string_val;
    // Add other types as needed for your grammar
}

%token <int_val> INTEGER
%token <double_val> FLOAT
%token <string_val> STRING

%type <int_val> expression
%type <double_val> term
%type <string_val> identifier
// Define types for other nonterminals as needed

%%
// Bison grammar rules go here
%%

// Additional C++ code, such as helper functions, can go here
```

In this example, the `%union` directive defines a union with three members: `int_val`, `double_val`, and `string_val`. The types of these members correspond to the types of semantic values that the grammar symbols can have. The `%token` and `%type` directives then specify which member of the union each token and nonterminal uses for its semantic value.

When you use a C++ parser with Bison, you can also use the `%define api.value.type variant` directive to use C++ `std::variant` instead of a union. This is a more modern and type-safe approach, but it requires C++17 or later. Here's an example:

```bison
%{
#include <string>
#include <variant>
// Include other necessary headers or declarations
%}

%define api.value.type variant

%token INTEGER "integer"
%token FLOAT "float"
%token STRING "string"

%type <int> expression
%type <double> term
%type <std::string> identifier
// Define types for other nonterminals as needed

%%
// Bison grammar rules go here
%%

// Additional C++ code, such as helper functions, can go here
```

In this case, Bison will automatically generate a `std::variant` type for `YYSTYPE` that can hold any of the specified types (`int`, `double`, `std::string`, etc.). You can then use `std::get` or `std::visit` to access the value within the variant.

Remember to update your Bison and C++ code to handle the `YYSTYPE` appropriately, whether you're using a union or a `std::variant`.

In Jinja2, pagination can be implemented by creating a macro that generates the necessary HTML for navigating between pages of content. A macro in Jinja2 is a reusable piece of template code that can be called with different parameters to produce output dynamically.

Here's a simple example of how you might create a pagination macro in Jinja2:

```jinja
{# Define the macro for pagination #}
{% macro render_pagination(page, total_pages, url_endpoint, **kwargs) %}
  <nav aria-label="Page navigation">
    <ul class="pagination">
      {# Previous Page Link #}
      <li class="page-item {% if page <= 1 %}disabled{% endif %}">
        <a class="page-link" href="{{ url_for(url_endpoint, page=page-1, **kwargs) if page > 1 else '#' }}" aria-label="Previous">
          <span aria-hidden="true">&laquo;</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      {# Page Number Links #}
      {% for p in range(1, total_pages + 1) %}
        <li class="page-item {% if p == page %}active{% endif %}">
          <a class="page-link" href="{{ url_for(url_endpoint, page=p, **kwargs) }}">{{ p }}</a>
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
      {# Next Page Link #}
      <li class="page-item {% if page >= total_pages %}disabled{% endif %}">
        <a class="page-link" href="{{ url_for(url_endpoint, page=page+1, **kwargs) if page < total_pages else '#' }}" aria-label="Next">
          <span aria-hidden="true">&raquo;</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
{% endmacro %}
```

To use this macro in your Jinja2 template, you would first import it and then call it with the appropriate arguments:

```jinja
{# Import the macro at the top of your template #}
{% from 'macros.html' import render_pagination with context %}

{# ... your template code ... #}

{# Call the macro to render pagination #}
{{ render_pagination(page=current_page, total_pages=total_number_of_pages, url_endpoint='your_view_function_name') }}
```

In this example, `current_page` would be the current page number, `total_number_of_pages` would be the total number of pages available, and `your_view_function_name` would be the name of the view function that handles the page requests. The `url_for` function is used to generate the URLs for the page links, and it's assumed that you're using Flask or a similar framework that provides this function.

The `**kwargs` allows you to pass additional arguments to the `url_for` function, which can be useful if your view function requires more parameters than just the page number.

Remember to adjust the macro to fit your specific needs, such as styling the pagination to match your site's design or adding more sophisticated page range logic to handle large numbers of pages.

