To check for CMake availability and version in a batch file, you can use the `cmake --version` command and parse its output. Below is an example of a batch script that performs these checks before attempting to generate a Visual Studio 2008 project using CMake.

```batchfile
@echo off
setlocal

:: Check for CMake availability
cmake --version >nul 2>&1
if errorlevel 1 (
    echo CMake is not installed or not found in the system PATH.
    exit /b 1
)

:: Extract the version of CMake and check if it meets the minimum required version
for /f "tokens=3" %%i in ('cmake --version ^| findstr /r /c:"^cmake version [0-9]*\.[0-9]*\.[0-9]*$"') do set CMAKE_VERSION=%%i

:: Define the minimum required version of CMake
set MIN_REQUIRED_VERSION=3.0.0

:: Compare the extracted version with the minimum required version
call :versionGreaterOrEqual %CMAKE_VERSION% %MIN_REQUIRED_VERSION% GEQ
if not %GEQ%==1 (
    echo The current CMake version %CMAKE_VERSION% is less than the minimum required version %MIN_REQUIRED_VERSION%.
    exit /b 1
)

:: Proceed with the CMake command to generate the project files
mkdir vs2008
cd vs2008
cmake -G "Visual Studio 9 2008" ..
if errorlevel 1 (
    echo Failed to generate Visual Studio 2008 project files with CMake.
    exit /b 1
)
pause
exit /b 0

:: Function to compare version numbers
:versionGreaterOrEqual
setlocal
set VERSION_A=%~1
set VERSION_B=%~2
set RESULT=0

for /f "tokens=1-3 delims=." %%a in ("%VERSION_A%") do (
    set /a MAJOR_A=%%a
    set /a MINOR_A=%%b
    set /a PATCH_A=%%c
)

for /f "tokens=1-3 delims=." %%a in ("%VERSION_B%") do (
    set /a MAJOR_B=%%a
    set /a MINOR_B=%%b
    set /a PATCH_B=%%c
)

if %MAJOR_A% GTR %MAJOR_B% (set RESULT=1) else (
    if %MAJOR_A% EQU %MAJOR_B% (
        if %MINOR_A% GTR %MINOR_B% (set RESULT=1) else (
            if %MINOR_A% EQU %MINOR_B% (
                if %PATCH_A% GEQ %PATCH_B% (set RESULT=1)
            )
        )
    )
)

endlocal & set %~3=%RESULT%
exit /b 0
```

This script first checks if CMake is available in the system PATH. If it is, it then checks the version of CMake against a specified minimum required version (in this case, 3.0.0). If the version is sufficient, it proceeds to create a directory and run the CMake command to generate Visual Studio 2008 project files. If any of these checks fail, it provides a meaningful error message and exits with an error code.

Please adjust the `MIN_REQUIRED_VERSION` variable to match the actual minimum version required by your project.

In Jinja2 templates, you can use `if`-`else` statements to control the flow of your template rendering based on conditions. The syntax is similar to Python's `if`-`else` statements but adapted for the templating environment. Here's how you can use `if`-else` statements in Jinja2:

```jinja
{% if condition %}
  <!-- Code to execute if the condition is true -->
{% elif another_condition %}
  <!-- Code to execute if the another_condition is true -->
{% else %}
  <!-- Code to execute if the conditions above are not met -->
{% endif %}
```

Here's an example of using `if`-`else` in a real template:

```jinja
{% if user.is_authenticated %}
  <p>Welcome back, {{ user.name }}!</p>
{% else %}
  <p>Please <a href="/login">log in</a> to access your account.</p>
{% endif %}
```

In this example, if `user.is_authenticated` evaluates to `True`, the template will render a welcome message. If it evaluates to `False`, it will render a prompt to log in.

You can also use `elif` to check for additional conditions:

```jinja
{% if temperature > 30 %}
  <p>It's really hot outside!</p>
{% elif temperature > 20 %}
  <p>It's a nice day.</p>
{% elif temperature > 10 %}
  <p>It's a bit chilly today.</p>
{% else %}
  <p>It's cold outside!</p>
{% endif %}
```

In this example, the template checks the `temperature` variable and renders different messages based on its value.

Remember that Jinja2 is whitespace-sensitive, so make sure to properly indent your code for readability, although the indentation does not affect the rendering of the template.

