To set up a base path for a RESTful API using configuration properties in Java, you typically use a combination of a properties file (like `application.properties` or `application.yml` if you're using Spring Boot) and annotations in your Java code to define the base path for your API.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do this using Spring Boot, which is a common framework for building RESTful APIs in Java:

1. **Define the Base Path in `application.properties` or `application.yml`:**

In your `src/main/resources/application.properties` file, you can define a custom property for the base path:

```properties
api.base-path=/api/v1
```

Or, if you prefer YAML format, in `src/main/resources/application.yml`:

```yaml
api:
  base-path: /api/v1
```

2. **Access the Property in Java Code:**

In your Spring Boot application, you can access this property using the `@Value` annotation or by using `@ConfigurationProperties`.

Using `@Value`:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ApiProperties {

    @Value("${api.base-path}")
    private String basePath;

    // Getter
    public String getBasePath() {
        return basePath;
    }
}
```

Using `@ConfigurationProperties`:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "api")
public class ApiProperties {

    private String basePath;

    // Getter and setter
    public String getBasePath() {
        return basePath;
    }

    public void setBasePath(String basePath) {
        this.basePath = basePath;
    }
}
```

3. **Set the Base Path in Your Controller:**

In your controller, you can now use the `ApiProperties` class to set the base path for your API endpoints.

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class MyApiController {

    private final ApiProperties apiProperties;

    @Autowired
    public MyApiController(ApiProperties apiProperties) {
        this.apiProperties = apiProperties;
    }

    @RequestMapping(apiProperties.getBasePath() + "/myendpoint")
    public String myEndpoint() {
        // Your endpoint logic here
        return "Hello, World!";
    }
}
```

However, note that using `@RequestMapping` with dynamic values like this won't work because the annotation values need to be constant expressions. Instead, you can set the base path for all controllers using a configuration class:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.EnableWebMvc;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.WebMvcConfigurer;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.PathMatchConfigurer;
import org.springframework.util.AntPathMatcher;

@Configuration
@EnableWebMvc
public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {

    private final ApiProperties apiProperties;

    @Autowired
    public WebConfig(ApiProperties apiProperties) {
        this.apiProperties = apiProperties;
    }

    @Override
    public void configurePathMatch(PathMatchConfigurer configurer) {
        AntPathMatcher matcher = new AntPathMatcher();
        matcher.setCaseSensitive(false);
        configurer.setPathMatcher(matcher);
        configurer.addPathPrefix(apiProperties.getBasePath(), c -> true);
    }
}
```

This configuration class will prepend the base path to all controllers in your application.

Remember to restart your application after making changes to the properties file to ensure the new configuration is loaded.

By following these steps, you can set up a base path for your RESTful API using configuration properties in a Java application, specifically with Spring Boot.

