Certainly! In Mathematica, `Reap` and `Sow` are used to collect data during a computation. `Sow` tags pieces of data, and `Reap` gathers them up. When used in a nested context, you can collect data at different levels of the computation. Here's an example using a nested loop structure:

```mathematica
(* Outer Reap to collect sums of numbers *)
result = Reap[
  Do[
    (* Inner Reap to collect individual numbers *)
    sum = Reap[
      Do[
        Sow[i*j, "Inner"], {j, 1, 5}
      ],
      "Inner"
    ];
    Sow[Total[First[sum]], "Outer"],
    {i, 1, 3}
  ],
  "Outer"
];

(* Extracting the results *)
innerResults = result[[2, 1, 1]];
outerResults = result[[1]];

(* Display the collected data *)
{innerResults, outerResults}
```

In this example, the inner loop is multiplying `i` and `j` and sowing the individual products with the tag `"Inner"`. The inner `Reap` collects these products and computes their sum. The outer loop then sows the sum of the products with the tag `"Outer"`. The outer `Reap` collects these sums.

The final result will be a list where the first element is the collection of individual numbers (products) and the second element is the collection of sums. The `First[sum]` is used to extract the actual list of sown values from the inner `Reap`, which returns a list where the first element is the list of sown values and the second element is a list of tags and associated values.

Please note that the tags `"Inner"` and `"Outer"` are used to differentiate the data collected at each level. They are optional, but they help to ensure that the correct data is collected at each level, especially when there are multiple `Sow` and `Reap` operations at different levels of the computation. If you omit the tags, Mathematica will collect all sown expressions at the nearest enclosing `Reap`.

